      "text": "Owing to the karst aquifers, Florida groundwaters contain high hardness and dissolved organic matter compared to those of many other states (USGS, 2005; [39] . The elevated concentrations of these constituents play critical roles in the remediation of CECs in water through various physicochemical processes [32] . For instance, calcium and other diva - lent cations may interact with PFAS anions, retaining them in the aqueous phase, thereby governing their fate and transport in water matrices [12] . Furthermore, high levels of ions and organic carbon may cause scaling and fouling of water treatment membranes and reduce their lifespan and also suppress the adsorption of contaminants like PFAS onto adsorption media [34] . Conventional water treatment processes are not specifically designed to remove PFAS from water due to their trace concentrations in water and resistance to degradation because of the strong C \u2013 F bond. Effective advanced PFAS treatment methods, including activated carbon filtra - tion, reverse osmosis (RO), biological treatment, UV irradiation, chlo - rine or ozone oxidation, and ion exchange have various limitations, including nonselectivity, frequent exhaustion, need for regeneration, high operational and maintenance cost, the formation of shorter-chain PFAS, undesired by-products, and so on [12] . Hence, a more efficient and economical solution to removing PFAS from contaminated water sources is required."
